# Cloudflare Pages headers for the Flutter web build.
# https://developers.cloudflare.com/pages/configuration/headers/
#
# ===========================================================================
# 🔴 THE STAMPED APP'S CONTENT-SECURITY-POLICY — ADDED 2026-09-12.
#
# The template shipped NO security headers at all until today, so every app
# stamped from it was born with none. `apps/subscriptiontracker` got its own on
# 2026-09-09 ([ADR 075]) and the template was not followed up — found by the
# factory-vs-app drift audit, research/factory-drift-2026-09-12/.
#
# 🔴 WHY IT IS NOT OPTIONAL HERE. Since [ADR 075] an app is published at
# `nikatru.com/<id>`, the SAME ORIGIN as the marketing site, the pricing page,
# `/checkout-return` and every other app — and the Supabase bearer token lives in
# that origin's web storage. An XSS in ANY app on the apex reaches all of it. A
# bundle with no policy does not inherit one: `_headers` is applied by the
# ASSET-serving stage (measured 2026-09-09 on a throwaway Pages project), so
# `sites/nikatru/_headers` never reaches a response the apex router returns from a
# proxy fetch, while THIS file's rules travel with the app's bytes and do.
#
# ⏱ THIS POLICY IS STRICTER THAN THE ONE `apps/subscriptiontracker` CARRIES, AND
# DELIBERATELY SO. That app's `script-src` lists a `'sha256-…'` for the one inline
# <script> in its index.html; the template's index.html has NO inline script (only
# `<script src="flutter_bootstrap.js" async>`), so a stamped app needs no hash and
# gets none. ⚠️ IF YOU ADD AN INLINE <script> TO index.html you MUST add its
# sha256 here in the same commit — assert-web-cache-policy.mjs recomputes every
# inline block's hash and fails when the header does not carry it. Without that,
# the block is blocked at runtime SILENTLY: no error page, no 404, just a script
# that never runs.
#
# Each unobvious entry, and why it is there:
#   · `'wasm-unsafe-eval'` — CanvasKit/skwasm. NOT `'unsafe-eval'`: the narrow
#     keyword permits WebAssembly compilation and nothing else.
#   · `worker-src`/`child-src blob:` — the engine spawns its wasm workers from
#     blob URLs.
#   · `challenges.cloudflare.com` — Turnstile, in script-src AND frame-src. It is
#     declared BEFORE `TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY` is ever set, so the gate cannot fail
#     closed on the day it is switched on.
#   · `connect-src` names the first-party hosts a stamped app calls, and the app's
#     OWN API host only when it has a backend. It does NOT name the apex, because
#     `'self'` IS the apex once the app is served there.
#   · the Supabase project is the ONE this portfolio shares (see `SUPABASE_URL` in
#     services/platform/wrangler.jsonc, shared across every NIKATRU app). ⏱
#     2026-09-24 — the auth cutover moves it to the self-hosted sign-in host
#     [ADR 080] names, `https://auth-api.nikatru.com`, and this line carries
#     BOTH hosts until the cutover is finished: the self-hosted one was added in
#     the cutover PREP, BEFORE `SUPABASE_URL` moves at Phase 5, so no stamped
#     app's sign-in is blocked by its own policy on switch day; the hosted
#     project stays as the rollback and is removed in Phase 6, when it is retired.
#
# ⚠️ `frame-ancestors 'none'` and `X-Frame-Options: DENY` are both set: the app must
# not be framed, including by another path on its own origin, because same-origin
# framing is otherwise unrestricted between app paths on one apex.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; base-uri 'self'; object-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; form-action 'self'; img-src 'self' data: blob:; font-src 'self' data:; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; script-src 'self' 'wasm-unsafe-eval' https://challenges.cloudflare.com; worker-src 'self' blob:; child-src 'self' blob:; frame-src 'self' https://challenges.cloudflare.com; connect-src 'self' https://lcrkiurkvzhkonjwhpiv.supabase.co https://auth-api.nikatru.com{{#needs_backend}} https://{{{api_domain}}}{{/needs_backend}} https://config.nikatru.com https://platform.nikatru.com https://glitchtip.nikatru.com; upgrade-insecure-requests
# ===========================================================================
#
# 🔴 WHY THIS FILE EXISTS — [pipeline 9]R-9, the cache limb.
#
# deploy-web.yml builds with `--pwa-strategy=none` ([ADR 023], LOCKED
# 2026-07-31: the service worker was rejected deliberately, it is not an
# oversight). So there is NO service worker and no client-side update
# machinery on the ONLY channel this factory serves. That leaves the HTTP
# cache as the entire update mechanism, and until this file existed nothing in
# the repository CHOSE a policy for it — `flutter build web` emits no
# `_headers` and the deploy ships `build/web` straight to Pages, so whatever
# the platform defaults to was what a returning visitor got.
#
# The failure that produces is not a slow update. It is a client running the
# PREVIOUS build with no way to know: the force-update gate (CFG-1) compares
# the version the app REPORTS, and a stale `flutter_bootstrap.js` reports the
# old one honestly. The kill-switch cannot see a client the cache is hiding
# from it.
#
# Flutter copies `web/` verbatim into `build/web`, so this file is deployed as
# a sibling of index.html and Pages reads it from the deployed directory.
#
# ⚠️ THE SPLIT IS BY WHETHER THE NAME CARRIES A HASH, not by file type. A
# STABLE name with CHANGING content must be revalidated every build; a name
# that changes when its bytes change can be cached forever, because a new
# build simply asks for a different name.
#
# `must-revalidate` with `max-age=0` is a conditional request, not a full
# download: the browser asks, the edge answers 304 when nothing moved. The
# cost of correctness here is one round trip.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 🔴 2026-08-04 — NOTHING A FLUTTER WEB BUILD EMITS IS CONTENT-ADDRESSED, AND
#    THIS TEMPLATE USED TO SAY IT WAS. `/assets/*`, `/canvaskit/*` and
#    `/icons/*` were declared `immutable, max-age=31536000` under the comment
#    "content-addressed output: the name changes when the bytes do" — inherited
#    from how a webpack-style build behaves, never verified against Flutter.
#
# MEASURED on the live web channel, not reasoned:
#
#   · `flutter_bootstrap.js` names `main.dart.js`, `canvaskit.js` and
#     `canvaskit.wasm` — plain and unhashed. A scan of the whole bootstrap for
#     a hash-shaped token returned NONE.
#   · The only `?v=` in it is on `flutter_service_worker.js`, which
#     `--pwa-strategy=none` never emits — so no asset request carries a
#     cache-busting query either.
#   · The real files under `/assets/` are `AssetManifest.bin.json`,
#     `FontManifest.json`, `NOTICES` and the bundled fonts: fixed names whose
#     CONTENT changes whenever the app's asset list does.
#   · `/icons/*` and `/favicon.png` are copied verbatim out of `web/`, so they
#     are whatever this repository last committed.
#
# `immutable` tells a browser not to revalidate even on an explicit reload, so
# on those paths it meant a returning visitor kept the previous launcher icon,
# font and asset manifest for up to a YEAR — the same failure this file exists
# to prevent, one directory down. This is the template every stamped app
# inherits, which is why the correction lives here and not only in one app.
#
# ⚠️ IF A FUTURE FLUTTER EMITS CONTENT-HASHED NAMES, THIS FLIPS BACK — for those
# names only, and the evidence for the flip is a fresh look at what
# `flutter_bootstrap.js` actually asks for, not this comment.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ⚠️ ONE RULE PER PATH, DELIBERATELY. Cloudflare Pages documents that "an
# incoming request which matches multiple rules' URL patterns will inherit all
# rules' headers", and that a header set twice is JOINED WITH A COMMA. Two
# rules that both match one path therefore produce
# `Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate, public, max-age=31536000,
# immutable` — a single self-contradicting header, not an override. The rules
# below are written as disjoint classes so that never arises.

# The stable-named entry points. A new deploy MUST be visible immediately.
/
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
/index.html
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
/flutter_bootstrap.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
/version.json
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
/manifest.json
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# main.dart.js is emitted under a STABLE name by `flutter build web`, so it is
# an entry point by the rule above even though it is compiled output.
/main.dart.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# Shipped verbatim from web/ and named in index.html. Stable name, changing
# content — a launcher-icon or favicon change must reach a returning visitor.
/favicon.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
/icons/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# Build output under a FIXED name: the asset and font manifests, NOTICES, the
# bundled fonts, and the CanvasKit engine (whose bytes track the Flutter SDK).
# None of these carry a hash, so none of them may be immutable.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
/canvaskit/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# The engine's text fallback fonts, copied into build/web/fallback-fonts/ by
# tooling/web/self-host-fallback-fonts.mjs (see web/flutter_bootstrap.js, which
# passes `fontFallbackBaseUrl`). Not shipped from web/, and the SAME rule as the
# bundle's other build output: a fixed name per engine version, so revalidate
# rather than assume immutability.
/fallback-fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
